<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Old Kedah</h1><p class="st-meta">300 CE – 1136 CE (approx.) · capital Bujang Valley</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The ancient trading kingdom of the Bujang Valley in Kedah, whose temple ruins are the oldest monumental remains in Malaysia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>From around the 4th century the estuary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merbok-river">Merbok River</a> became a landfall for ships crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallava-script">Pallava script</a>, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhagupta-stone">Buddhagupta stone</a> naming a sea captain, show Indian merchants settling there. Indian texts call the port Kataha and Tamil sources Kadaram.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Over fifty candi, brick and laterite shrines both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist, have been found in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujang-valley">Bujang Valley</a>, along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-technology">ironworking</a> sites at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sungai-batu">Sungai Batu</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah</a> exported forest goods and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> and redistributed Chinese, Persian and Indian wares. From the 7th century it was closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a>, whose ruler was captured at Kadaram during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> raid of 1025.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> attacks the kingdom recovered but declined as trade routes shifted. According to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kedah-sultanate">Kedah</a> annals, its ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-ong-mahawangsa">Phra Ong Mahawangsa</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 1136, taking the name Mudzaffar Shah and founding the Kedah Sultanate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bujang-valley">Bujang Valley</a> sites are now a major archaeological park.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/old-kedah.jpg" alt="Old Kedah" width="320" height="207"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:OSBORN(1860)_p374_TORNADO_OFF_THE_COAST_OF_QUEDAH.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>300 CE – 1136 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bujang Valley</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Phra Ong Mahawangsa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malaysia">Malaysia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kadaram, Kataha, Kedah Tua</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=718&amp;focus=old-kedah" aria-label="Old Kedah on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7463 1963 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Old Kedah lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7783 2219L7833 2434M7783 2219L7187 2016M7783 2219L7780 2205"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7833 2434h0M7187 2016h0M7780 2205h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7783 2219h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7783 2219h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bujang Valley. <a href="/atlas?year=718&amp;focus=old-kedah">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
